          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "Major cardiology guidance describes four drug classes as first-line for most adults without a separate reason to choose something else. These are thiazide-type diuretics, ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs), and dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers (*Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*). The same guidance says an ACE inhibitor and an ARB should not be combined, because the combination adds harm without adding benefit. The retrieved text also says treatment usually begins with two agents when blood pressure is well above target, but the passage is cut off before it gives the exact cut-off.\n\nAuthorities differ on the starting point. The United States uses 130/80 mm Hg as the diagnostic threshold. European guidance starts office hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and treats 130–139 / 80–89 as high-normal rather than stage 1. Because of this, the same reading can be labeled differently, and the group treated as hypertensive is much larger under one convention. European guidance advises lifestyle measures alone for much of the population that United States guidance calls stage 1 (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*; *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*). I can't say which convention is right here. Your clinician can tell you which one applies to you.\n\nI could not find evidence-strength grades for these drug classes in the retrieved material. The documents describe what is recommended, not how strong the supporting trials are, so I can't give a grade from these sources.\n\nThe choice of drug depends on your other health conditions and on discussion with your clinician, so please don't start or change any medication based on this summary.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
